===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Any place which is [[optimum#Adjective|optimum]] for a certain [[action]] to [[occur]].
#* {{quote-journal|author=Mariam Sodergren|title=Private Lessons: Third in a Series: Better Balance|magazine=[[w:Skiing (magazine)|Skiing]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Times Mirror Company|Times Mirror Magazines]]|month=January|year=1991|volume=43|issue=5|page=113|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=hgQGPAwqsfIC&pg=PA113|column=3|issn=0037-6264|oclc=612061178|passage=Here's a way to start improving your balance right now. Let's tune into a reference point, the place on which you can feel your weight concentrated, the spot that will always let you know whether or not you are in balance. I call this reference point the "'''sweet spot'''" of your foot. The '''sweet spot''' is just behind the big toe and to the side of the ball of each foot (some people grow bunions on this point).|lang=en}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Arun Sinha|chapter=|title=Sweet Spot: How to Maximize Marketing for Business Growth|location=Hoboken, N.J.|publisher={{w|John Wiley & Sons}}|year=2007|page=77|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=hZh40U9Pvi8C&pg=PA77|isbn=978-0-470-05143-6|passage=Business hit a '''sweet spot'''—''a place, time, or experience in which a company's brands, products and services, finances, leaders, and employees are in tune and in time with consumer neds, aspirations, and budgets''—''only'' when (no matter how global, or gigantic, the company) everything and everyone is aligned. '''Sweet spot''' companies practice full-circle marketing, which means that ''everyone'' must become part of the marketing team.}}
#* {{quote-journal|author=Barney Ronay|title={{w|Antonio Conte}}’s brilliance has turned [[w:Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]]’s pop-up team into champions|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2017/may/13/antonio-conte-chelsea-champions-manager|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170909101122/https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2017/may/13/antonio-conte-chelsea-champions-manager|archivedate=9 September 2017|newspaper={{w|The Guardian}}|location=London|date=13 May 2017|passage=Six months old, a half-season project, they are already more watchable and more coherent than the second phase of the title-winning team of two years ago; but not at the level of the luminous, steamrollering [[w:José Mourinho|[José] Mourinho]] Mk1 team, a rare concurrence of prime-cut talent and a manager in the '''sweet spot''' of his own powers.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|euphemistic}} The [[clitoris]], [[prostate gland]], or other center of [[sexual]] [[pleasure#Noun|pleasure]].
# {{lb|en|physics|slang}} The center of [[percussion]].
#* {{quote-book|author=Bernard Brogliato|chapter=Two Bodies Colliding|editors=E. D. Sontag and M. Thoma|title=Nonsmooth Mechanics: Models, Dynamics and Control|series=Communications and Control Engineering|edition=2nd|location=London; Berlin; Heidelberg|publisher=[[w:Springer Science+Business Media|Springer-Verlag]]|year=1999|section=section 4.1.4 (The Percussion Center)|page=116|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=qKCdH7HklEwC&pg=PA116|isbn=978-1-85233-143-6|passage=The center of percussion also finds more exotic applications, like in tennis dynamics: the so-called '''''sweet spot''''' {{...}} s a special impact point on the racket strings used to prevent jarring of the hand. It is defined either as a vibration node, or as the center of percussion, or as the point where the restitution coefficient {{...}} is maximum and vibrations minimum{{nb...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=John D[avid] Barrow|authorlink=John D. Barrow|chapter=Cushioning the Blow|title=Mathletics: A Scientist Explains 100 Amazing Things about Sport|location=London|publisher={{w|Vintage Books}}|year=2013|isbn=978-0-09-958423-0|passage=Most of us are familiar with the existence of a ‘'''sweet spot'''’ on a tennis racket or bat: the place where it is best to strike the ball. {{...}} Physicists call this '''sweet spot''' the ‘centre of percussion’ and it occurs at a distance from the top of a cricket or baseball bat that is about two thirds of its total length.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Thomas Jay Smith|author2=Robert Henning|author3=Michael G. Wade|author4=Thomas Fisher|chapter=Variability in Human Motor and Sport Performance|title=Variability in Human Performance|location=Boca Raton, Fla.|publisher={{w|CRC Press}}|year=2015|section=section 2.1 (Introduction)|page=31|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=j2bSBQAAQBAJ&pg=PA31|isbn=978-1-4665-7971-2|passage=Club heads [of golf clubs] have weight distributions designed to optimize both launch angle of the ball and minimizing the effects of off-center shots that do not precisely impact the so-called '''sweet spot''' (the center of percussion).}}
# {{lb|en|sports}} The [[optimal]] [[place#Noun|place]] on a [[bat#Noun|bat]], racquet, etc., with which to [[hit#Verb|hit]] a [[ball#Noun|ball]], resulting in the latter [[rebound]]ing with the [[maximum]] possible [[velocity]].
#: {{ux|en|He hit the gapper right off of the '''sweet spot'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Howard Brody|chapter=The Sweet Spots of a Tennis Racket|title=Tennis Science for Tennis Players|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher={{w|University of Pennsylvania Press}}|year=1987|page=23|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=iTadg2mJRF0C&pg=PA23|isbn=978-0-8122-1238-9|passage=When you hit a shot and it really feels good, you claim that you have hit the '''sweet spot'''. But can this feeling be quantified? {{...}} Do some rackets have a '''sweet spot''' that is sweeter than that of other rackets, or is the size of the '''sweet spot''' the only relevant consideration?}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Ed[ward] Turner|author2=[Brent] Woody Clouse|chapter=|title=Winning Racquetball: Skills, Drills, and Strategies|location=Champaign, Ill.; Windsor, Ont.|publisher=Human Kinetics|year=1995|page=10|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=j59MFV8J-d8C&pg=PA10|isbn=978-0-87322-721-6|passage=There are actually ''three'' '''sweet spots''' on a racquet's face. Each '''sweet spot''' measures a different physical characteristic of your racquet. The '''sweet spot''' that most of us think of is the place on the strings where the ball rebounds with the most power, or, technically, the post on the racquet with the maximum coefficient of restitution. The second '''sweet spot''' is where the least amount of shock is given to your hand and arm. This is known as the center of percussion. The third '''sweet spot''' is the node or place where the least amount of vibration occurs after the initial ball impact. Normally all three '''sweet spots''' do not occur in the same location.}}

